WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.920
- Thanks to Skillshare for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.050
Learn to think like a lawyer
for free for two months

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.890
by clicking the link in the description.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.330
The president has declared
a national emergency

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.500
and is using that as
justification to build his wall.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.827
Can he do that?

00:00:14.827 --> 00:00:18.770
(orchestral musical flourish)

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:22.590
In ancient Rome, the dictator
was a temporary position

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.850
given to the head of state
in times of emergency.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.730
So giving emergency power to the president

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.560
is not a new idea, but there's nothing

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.870
like that in the Constitution.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:36.200
The Constitution makes no
provision for a national emergency

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.230
and does not recognize
anything like emergency powers.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.940
But some argue that the president

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.690
has been delegated those
powers by Congress.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:44.640
So today, we're gonna talk

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.300
about President Trump's
emergency declaration.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.900
Number one, I wanna talk
about what emergency powers

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.300
the president does actually have.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.140
Number two, I wanna talk
about whether there is,

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.910
in fact, an emergency that allows

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:57.950
the president to invoke those powers.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.970
Number three, I wanna
talk about what powers

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.770
Congress has and which it has
bestowed on the president,

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.530
and number four, I wanna talk
about the possible outcomes

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.260
that may come as a result
of this national emergency

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:12.740
declaration and what that
means for future presidents.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.370
In other words, are we going to see a lot

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.910
of new declarations of national
emergencies over things

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.710
like the environment, gun
control, or healthcare.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.390
So let's talk about the first question.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.010
What emergency powers does
the president have, and can

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:27.380
the president act outside
of those enumerated powers?

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.010
Well, with few exceptions, the president

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:32.230
can only act with
Congressional authorization.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.410
The president has broad executive
and enforcement authority

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.830
to use his or her discretion to determine

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:41.160
how to enforce the law or
how to otherwise manage

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.400
the resources of the executive branch,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.180
but the president has
virtually no authority

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.350
to pass laws or appropriate
funds without Congress.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.110
And in fact, in the famous case

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:54.680
of Youngstown Sheet and
Tube Company v. Sawyer,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.900
the Supreme Court ruled that
President Harry Truman's

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.140
attempt to nationalize US
steel mills during a strike

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.760
in the Korean war was in
fact unconstitutional.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.050
So as one Congressperson has said,

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.450
if Harry Truman couldn't
nationalize the steel industry

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.910
during wartime, this president
doesn't have the power

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.670
to declare an emergency and build

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.270
a multi-billion-dollar wall on the border.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:17.480
Now, one of the first fundamental
issues that is implicated

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.460
by this emergency declaration
is the appropriations issue.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.630
According to Article
One of the Constitution,

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:27.140
only Congress can
appropriate federal funds.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:29.980
Article One, Section One of
the US Constitution states

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.600
that all legislative
powers herein are granted

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.040
and shall be vested in the Congress.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.610
Article One, Section
Eight states that Congress

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.010
has the exclusive
spending power to, quote,

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.200
provide for the general
welfare of the United States.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.670
Lawsuits have already been filed stating

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.260
that President Trump has
violated the United States

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.200
Constitution's separation
of powers doctrine

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.520
by taking executive action
to fund a border wall

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:58.590
without Congress actually
appropriating the correct funding.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.990
Those lawsuits have argued that
the 2019 Appropriations Act

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.610
is an explicit denial of the president's

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.510
requested funds for the border wall.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.930
Those lawsuits also
argue that the president

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.320
has violated the separation
of powers doctrine

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.880
by unilaterally diverting
funds that Congress

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.040
has already appropriate for other things

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.100
that are not related to the border wall,

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.000
and Article One, Section Nine
of the Constitution states

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.700
that no money shall be
drawn from the treasury

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.630
but in consequence of
appropriations made by law.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.470
The lawsuits argue that Congress heard

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:35.760
the president's request and
explicitly rejected the request

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.070
to appropriate funds to build
any kind of border wall.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:42.100
So the recent attorney's
general lawsuit argues

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.550
that President Trump violated

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.280
the appropriations clause
of the Constitution

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.090
by funding construction for
a border wall with funds

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.580
that were not appropriated
for that purpose.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.560
But here, the Trump
administration will counter

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:56.290
that Congress has, in fact, passed a law

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.560
that gives him authority to do this.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.650
A number of laws do in fact
authorize the president

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.910
to take certain actions by
declaring a national emergency.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.350
The primary law the president
seems to be operating

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.990
under is the National
Emergencies Act of 1976,

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:13.270
as amended in 1985, which governs

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.660
the president's declaration
of a national emergency.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:20.730
Now, under the NEA, the
president must specifically state

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.860
the law under which he proposes to act,

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.820
and he still has to comply with
the provisions of that law.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.430
This is incredibly important,
because the redesignated funds

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.560
must still comply with the original law

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.180
under which the funds were appropriated.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.380
The first preliminary
issue is can the president

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.890
even invoke the NEA in
these circumstances?

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:44.380
The question is, is there an
emergency, and it also depends

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.230
on what the definition of an emergency is.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:49.470
The term emergency is not defined

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.070
in the National Emergencies
Act, and this fact

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:54.220
cuts in a couple of different directions.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:55.530
On the one hand, some argue

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.170
that this gives the
president carte blanche

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:00.470
to declare anything an
emergency, because the NEA

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:03.570
doesn't specify what
constitutes an emergency.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.490
But I think that misses the point.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.840
One of the jobs of the courts

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.800
from time immemorial is
to provide definitions

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:15.240
where a particular statute
is silent or ambiguous.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.950
In the case of federal
laws, courts often look

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:21.040
to the intent of Congress
to impute a definition

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.950
to an otherwise undefined
term, and let's be clear,

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.900
there is no emergency as most
people understand that word.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.800
Merriam-Webster's defines
emergency as a, quote,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.100
unforeseen combination of circumstances

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:37.500
of the resulting state that
calls for immediate action.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:39.670
President Trump has said himself

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:42.140
that this was not strictly necessary.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:43.830
He could have gone
through regular channels,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.380
but he wanted to do if faster.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:46.900
- I wanna do it faster.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.930
I could do the wall over
a longer period of time.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:50.930
I didn't need to do this.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:52.980
But I'd rather do it much faster.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.330
- And in the present day,

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:57.920
illegal immigration is
at near historic lows.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:01.090
The president may not be
satisfied with those numbers,

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:03.010
but as most people understand

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:05.260
the lay definition of emergency,

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.870
it doesn't seem like there's
any against that it fits.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:09.390
It seems very likely that this

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.730
is not the kind of situation
that Congress had in mind

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:14.970
when passing the 1976
National Emergencies Act.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.160
In fact, that particular
law was an attempt

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.450
to curtail presidential power

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:22.230
that had been exercised by
the Nixon administration.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.960
So it is possible that the
court will defer to whatever

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.810
the president declares
as a national emergency.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.800
I don't think the courts
that look at this will do so,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.340
in part because of the
political ramifications.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.930
It might allow future presidents

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:37.290
to declare whatever they
want as an emergency.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.050
But that being said, it isn't uncommon

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:43.050
for courts to have to
weigh in on the definitions

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:47.010
of terms in various laws, and if the court

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:50.100
uses a lay definition of emergency,

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.900
then it's likely that
this particular situation

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:56.120
would not qualify, and
that may end the analysis

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.360
of this particular legal
question right there.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.050
But let's assume for the sake of argument

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:02.740
that this does qualify
as a national emergency,

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:05.600
or the court takes a
deferential view that says

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.290
basically anything can
be a national emergency.

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:11.550
Now that means that we have
to look at the second part.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:15.770
What laws are invoked in order
to reappropriate the funds,

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:16.960
because, as I mentioned before,

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.450
the president still has to
comply with the underlying laws

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.320
that passed the funds in the first place.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:25.470
One of the laws the
president is relying on

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.970
to see the lion's share
of the billions of dollars

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:30.610
he's asking for border wall construction

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:32.690
deals with military construction.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:37.410
Under this military construction
law, 33 U.S.C. 2293,

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.900
the president may terminate
or defer military construction

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.570
that he has deemed not essential
to the National Defense,

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:45.680
and may apply those
resources for a project

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.410
to construct another project
which he considers essential.

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:51.560
But the reappropriated funds
under this particular law

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:54.550
must still constitute
military construction,

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:57.600
and given the fact that we
are not at war with Mexico,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.790
it makes it a hard sell to
claim that a border wall

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:03.350
is a military construction project.

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:05.820
In addition, this particular
construction project

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.520
does not require the
armed forces to build,

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.650
and in fact, there's
an argument to be made

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:13.830
that the military cannot be used to build

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.540
this particular project under
the Posse Comitatus rule.

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:19.260
Posse Comitatus is a
doctrine that is enshrined

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:21.470
in statutory law in the
United States that says

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:24.980
you cannot use the military
for domestic projects,

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:26.620
especially not enforcement.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:29.680
You might recall that when
President Trump sent troops

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.890
to the border to fight
against an alleged caravan

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:36.910
of potential immigrants and refugees,

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:39.620
they weren't actually send
to the border to fight

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.860
but really to only provide backup services

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:45.100
to the Department of Homeland Security.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:48.260
That was largely because of the
doctrine of Posse Comitatus,

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.840
which says that you cannot
use armed forces domestically.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.540
So even if the president is
able to get over the hurdle

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:58.150
of declaring a national emergency,
he's still going to have

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:01.280
a number of hurdles with
respect to the underlying law

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:03.960
from which he is taking
the appropriated funds.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.250
Now, on the other hand, the
government will probably argue

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:08.470
that the courts should
give broad deference

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:10.530
to the president's
determination that there is

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:13.400
a national emergency,
and also broad deference

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:15.950
as to what constitutes
military construction.

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:19.200
As the Commander-in-Chief
of the armed forces,

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.700
we don't often want to
be able to second-guess

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:24.980
the Commander-in-Chief's
ability to declare

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:27.830
what is essential to the
national defense and what isn't.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:31.560
So if this case gets to the
Supreme Court and isn't bounced

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.080
on some technicality,
it's completely unclear

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:37.130
which way the Supreme
Court might go on this.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:41.340
There are long strings of
cases that are deferential

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:43.990
to the president and the
president's authority

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:47.090
to act where the Constitution allows it.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:49.910
So this is certainly not a
slam-dunk for either side,

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:53.170
and this is also one of those
areas where the traditional

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:56.290
cleavages of liberal versus
conservative justices

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:59.500
on the Supreme Court
don't always make sense.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:01.730
Sometimes, the conservative justices

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:05.090
vote to curtail executive
power, and sometimes,

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:08.150
the more liberal members of
the Supreme Court take the view

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:11.230
that the executive powers
should be very expansive.

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:14.880
So when it comes to who might
view this as an overreach

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.930
and who might view this as
a perfectly reasonable thing

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:20.250
for a president to do, all bets are off.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:22.470
I should note, however,
that litigation over this

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:24.750
emergency declaration
is going to have issues

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:26.490
with standing and ripeness.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:29.190
This is always a perennial
issue when you have

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:32.850
these kinds of acts and
political questions,.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.300
In order to file a lawsuit,

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:35.600
you have to have what's called standing,

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:38.220
which means that you have
to be personally aggrieved

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.100
in order to file suit,
and in terms of standing,

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.750
individual members of
Congress do not have standing,

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:46.330
but sometimes Congress as a body does.

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:48.790
Sometimes affected states have standing;

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:49.970
sometimes they don't.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:51.470
There's also the issue of ripeness.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:55.630
Since only the declaration
has been made and no action

00:10:55.630 --> 00:10:58.080
has actually been taken, it's not clear

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.880
that this case is actually
ripe for adjudication,

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:04.040
but the recent litigation
in California which deals

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:08.370
with dozens of states, dozens
of states' attorneys general,

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.790
and several farmers who are likely

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:13.600
to be affected by this
emergency declaration

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:16.830
looks to solve some of those
standing and ripeness issues,

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:20.040
but no matter what, this
will have huge implications

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:22.770
for future presidential actions.

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:25.430
Those on both sides of the
aisle are right to worry

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.950
about the president in
this particular setting.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.330
When the Senate passed the
National Emergencies Act,

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:32.730
it stated in the notes of passage

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:34.840
that the NEA was designed to ensure

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:38.000
that the president's
extraordinary emergency powers

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:42.090
would be utilized only when
emergencies actually exist.

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.870
Under the National Emergencies
Act, Congress has 18 days

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:48.300
to adopt a joint resolution
to terminate the declaration,

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:51.760
but that joint resolution is
subject to a presidential veto.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.040
A recent Supreme Court
authority has stated that

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:57.190
a presidential veto can only
be overturned by a two-thirds

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:00.880
vote in both houses, and
this is incredibly unlikely.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.700
So that means that if the court upholds

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:07.030
the Trump administration's
declaration of emergency here,

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:09.470
the likely outcome is
that the next president,

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:11.690
and potentially the president after that,

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:14.020
is likely to be able to
declare almost anything

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:16.660
a national emergency in that case as well,

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.860
and at least in my opinion, that seems

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:21.000
like exactly what the
framers of the Constitution

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.330
tried to avoid when they gave

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:26.500
the exclusive right to pass
laws and the exclusive right

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.160
to make appropriations only to Congress.

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:31.450
President Trump made a
major mistake by saying

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:33.920
that he didn't need to issue
the declaration of emergency

